WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.850
- The earthquakes that shook
Turkey and Syria this month

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.140
killing thousands,

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.390
have fractured the country's
transport networks.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.150
The 7.8 magnitude
earthquake destroyed roads

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.280
and collapsed buildings onto roads,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.080
rendering some routes impassable,

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.320
and effectively rewriting the
map for some cities overnight.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.210
That's made it harder for rescue workers

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.370
to get aid in and the injured out.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.330
But now, aid workers

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.850
have a number of technological
tools at their disposal

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.710
to find new paths through the rubble.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.090
Here's how mapping technology

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.590
could help the rescue
efforts in Turkey and Syria.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.580
Firstly, there's drones.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.750
It might seem obvious,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.180
but drones can instantly
give those on the ground

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.910
a real-time bird's eye
view of a disaster site,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.200
helping aid workers identify

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.540
the safest or easiest
route through obstacles

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.600
and informing them of which
paths are completely impassable.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.890
The visuals can also be used

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.810
to help rescuers identify areas

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.610
that might not have been searched,

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.030
and even potentially spot signs
of life among the wreckage.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.440
What's more, they can be deployed

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.350
to try and assess the structural
integrity of a building,

00:00:58.350 --> 00:00:59.790
giving rescuers an indication

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.740
of whether it might be safe to proceed.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.290
Spain has said it is
sending drones to Turkey

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.180
as part of its aid package.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.730
But to get a bigger picture
of how road infrastructure

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.410
might have been damaged by a quake,

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.990
you need to go higher, much higher.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.790
Enter Copernicus.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:15.780
The Copernicus Program

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.420
is the European Union's
eyes on planet Earth,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.650
and is funded by the Union

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.380
to monitor things like floods, lava flows,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.420
wildfires and earthquakes.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.040
In addition to their own sensors,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.710
the group sources high
resolution satellite images

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.830
from companies like Airbus,
Maxar and Planet Labs,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.900
and combines them with data and analysis

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.990
to create a new map of a
region in under two days.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.140
See, mere hours after the
Turkey-Syria earthquake struck,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.640
the Red Cross activated

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.550
the European Union's
Emergency Mapping Service,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.170
and Copernicus began analyzing

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.540
satellite images of affected areas.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.420
Through a combination of
algorithms and human analysis,

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.980
the group has been able to identify

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.360
roads that have been destroyed

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.920
and buildings that have collapsed.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.690
That open source information

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.630
is then published for all to
see and updated regularly,

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.750
giving rescue workers a fresh
map of their surroundings.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.120
But what if you could
predict which areas of a city

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.330
were most likely to be cut off
in the event of an earthquake

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.100
so you could reinforce those areas

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.800
to try and make sure
they remained connected?

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.000
Well, that modeling tool

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.040
is exactly what engineering researchers

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.910
Dr. Ji-Eun Byun and Dina
D'Ayala have tried to develop.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.000
The pair worked with Istanbul's
Metropolitan Municipality

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.880
to develop a model for
evaluating which areas

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.590
are most at risk of being cut off

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.180
in the event of an earthquake.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.030
To do this, they took data on
things like building density,

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.850
seismic strength, vehicle
throughput and road layout

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.950
to create a heat map, which not only shows

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.560
which roads and buildings
might be damaged in a quake,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.630
but also which roads might become blocked

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.070
if a building collapsed,

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.860
and crucially, which
areas were most at risk

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.770
from being entirely cut off
in the event of an earthquake.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.440
The research could also be
used to show where investment

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.960
could increase resilience
against earthquakes,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.180
essentially, whether it
would be a better investment

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.490
to strengthen a building
or strengthen the the road,

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.500
or add more connectivity to an area.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.480
Now, the study was only
published last year,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.990
and the researchers tell me it
takes a lot of granular data

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.610
in order for the model to work.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.443
But in the future,

00:03:09.443 --> 00:03:11.490
it could potentially be
a tool that cities use

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.659
to try and keep a path clear
in the event of an earthquake.

00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:17.909
(bright gentle music)

